    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d635">

                <pb facs="0791" n="667" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg667"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">818.6363 Am 6/114: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Costa Rica</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Thurston</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >San José</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-01-13T09:00:00-06:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 13,
                                1922—9 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received January 14—3:42
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>144. My 139, December 14, 10 a.m. British Minister and Costa Rican Government
                    last evening reached an agreement concerning convention requiring approval of
                    Congress under which Amory <pb facs="0792" n="668" xml:id="pg668"/> and Royal
                    Bank claims are to be submitted to arbitration of Chief Justice Taft.<anchor
                        n="25" xml:id="d635-f1"/> British Minister left this morning for Panama.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Thurston</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d635-f1" n="25" xml:id="d635-f1-content">
                <p>The English text of the amended Alvarado-Bennett convention is printed in <hi
                        rendition="#italic">British and Foreign State Papers</hi>, vol. 116 (1922),
                    p. 438.</p>
                <p>For the award rendered by Chief Justice Taft at Washington, Oct. 18, 1923, see
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Fallo Arbitral del Chief Justice de los Estados
                        Unidos de America sobre las Reclamaciones del Royal Bank of Canada y de John
                        M. Amory &amp; Son</hi>, San José, Costa Rica, Imprenta Nacional, 1924.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
